17,66,1124,5187,1124,26888,90,16,19,19,66,61,17,3417,90,16,15,92,284,1124,37018,90,17,66,1124,5187,220,16,17,66,15170,16,15,92,7110,921,1986,6696,601,1378,9904,510,78045,65,284,1124,37018,90,16,19,66,15170,16,15,92,284,1124,37018,90,22,66,15170,20,92,1124,31610,1124,1318,96065,1921,458,7546,9139,1124,31610,1124,1318,90,269,92,1124,31610,293,284,1124,37018,19999,16,15,66,15170,16,15,92,284,481,66,1124,31610,1124,1318,96065,1921,3204,2474,3108,28316,525,6785,9139,7110,921,44500,11,279,1172,3204,7546,6291,374,510,78045,65,284,1124,37018,90,22,66,15170,20,92,1124,1318,90,320,11662,11,773,335,220,20,1124,16106,272,701,1124,6383,550,272,284,220,20,74,1124,1318,90,369,1045,7546,335,595,7110,921,12209,17767,65,284,220,22,74,59,701,323,31334,10607,17767,66,284,220,20,74,57758,323,17767,65,284,220,22,74,57758,1119,17767,64,284,1124,37018,90,65,49138,15170,17,92,284,1124,37018,90,22,74,488,220,20,74,15170,17,92,284,220,21,74,59,49555,522,37420,91339,29,151645,198,151644,872,198,23526,504,279,74003,13,9258,1172,279,41171,13,3155,537,17024,279,6291,13,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nIn triangle $ABC,$ the length of side $\\overline{BC}$ is equal to the average of the other two sides.  Also,\n\\[\\cos C = \\frac{AB}{AC}.\\]Given that all the side lengths are integers, find the smallest possible area of triangle $ABC.$\n\nPartial draft:\n<partial_draft>\nTo solve the problem, we start by defining the side lengths of triangle \\(ABC\\) as \\(a = BC\\), \\(b = AC\\), and \\(c = AB\\). We are given two conditions: \\(a = \\frac{b+c}{2}\\) and \\(\\cos C = \\frac{c}{b}\\).\n\nFirst, we express the first condition algebraically:\n\\[a = \\frac{b+c}{2} \\implies 2a = b + c.\\]\n\nUsing the Law of Cosines for angle \\(C\\), we have:\n\\[\\cos C = \\frac{a^2 + b^2 - c^2}{2ab}.\\]\nGiven \\(\\cos C = \\frac{c}{b}\\), we substitute to get:\n\\[\\frac{c}{b} = \\frac{a^2 + b^2 - c^2}{2ab}.\\]\nMultiplying both sides by \\(2ab\\), we obtain:\n\\[2ac = a^2 + b^2 - c^2.\\]\nRearranging terms, we get:\n\\[a^2 + b^2 - 2ac - c^2 = 0.\\]\n\nNow, we substitute \\(a = \\frac{b+c}{2}\\) into the equation:\n\\[\\left(\\frac{b+c}{2}\\right)^2 + b^2 - 2\\left(\\frac{b+c}{2}\\right)c - c^2 = 0.\\]\nSimplifying the left side, we get:\n\\[\\frac{(b+c)^2}{4} + b^2 - bc - c^2 - c^2 = 0 \\implies \\frac{b^2 + 2bc + c^2}{4} + b^2 - bc - 2c^2 = 0.\\]\nMultiplying through by 4 to clear the fraction, we get:\n\\[b^2 + 2bc + c^2 + 4b^2 - 4bc - 8c^2 = 0 \\implies 5b^2 - 2bc - 7c^2 = 0.\\]\nThis is a quadratic equation in \\(b\\):\n\\[5b^2 - 2bc - 7c^2 = 0.\\]\nWe solve this quadratic equation using the quadratic formula \\(b = \\frac{-B \\pm \\sqrt{B^2 - 4AC}}{2A}\\), where \\(A = 5\\), \\(B = -2c\\), and \\(C = -7c^2\\):\n\\[b = \\frac{2c \\pm \\sqrt{4c^2 + 140c^2}}{10} = \\frac{2c \\pm \\sqrt{144c^2}}{10} = \\frac{2c \\pm 12c}{10}.\\]\nThis gives us two solutions:\n\\[b = \\frac{14c}{10} = \\frac{7c}{5} \\quad \\text{(not an integer)} \\quad \\text{or} \\quad b = \\frac{-10c}{10} = -c \\quad \\text{(not possible since side lengths are positive)}.\\]\nThus, the only possible integer solution is:\n\\[b = \\frac{7c}{5} \\text{ (integer, so } 5 \\mid c), \\implies c = 5k \\text{ for some integer } k.\\]\nThen \\(b = 7k\\), and substituting \\(c = 5k\\) and \\(b = 7k\\) into \\(a = \\frac{b+c}{2} = \\frac{7k + 5k}{2} = 6k\\).\n\n\n</partial_draft>","role":"user"},{"content":"Continue from the interruption. Output only the continuation. Do not restart the solution.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"5bf04100f3e65e1145996ff5ad266b9d34896c663ed102cd8ed7487623f00a9e"},"seconds":16.360620975494385,"seed":200209557876862982,"stop":"eos","text":"We have the side lengths as \\(a = 6k\\), \\(b = 7k\\), and \\(c = 5k\\). Since \\(k\\) is a positive integer, the smallest possible value for \\(k\\) is 1, giving the side lengths \\(a = 6\\), \\(b = 7\\), and \\(c = 5\\).\n\nTo find the area of the triangle, we use Heron's formula. First, we calculate the semi-perimeter \\(s\\):\n\\[s = \\frac{a + b + c}{2} = \\frac{6 + 7 + 5}{2} = 9.\\]\nThen, the area \\(K\\) is given by:\n\\[K = \\sqrt{s(s-a)(s-b)(s-c)} = \\sqrt{9(9-6)(9-7)(9-5)} = \\sqrt{9 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 4} = \\sqrt{216} = 6\\sqrt{6}.\\]\nTherefore, the smallest possible area of triangle \\(ABC\\) is:\n\\[\\boxed{6\\sqrt{6}}.\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{6\\sqrt{6}}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":false},"graded_text":"We have the side lengths as \\(a = 6k\\), \\(b = 7k\\), and \\(c = 5k\\). Since \\(k\\) is a positive integer, the smallest possible value for \\(k\\) is 1, giving the side lengths \\(a = 6\\), \\(b = 7\\), and \\(c = 5\\).\n\nTo find the area of the triangle, we use Heron's formula. First, we calculate the semi-perimeter \\(s\\):\n\\[s = \\frac{a + b + c}{2} = \\frac{6 + 7 + 5}{2} = 9.\\]\nThen, the area \\(K\\) is given by:\n\\[K = \\sqrt{s(s-a)(s-b)(s-c)} = \\sqrt{9(9-6)(9-7)(9-5)} = \\sqrt{9 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 4} = \\sqrt{216} = 6\\sqrt{6}.\\]\nTherefore, the smallest possible area of triangle \\(ABC\\) is:\n\\[\\boxed{6\\sqrt{6}}.\\]","id":"precalculus:97"}
